From cf17d19224770b298d95d4ee94c5b94ad4fe61d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DogukanDG <dagtekindogukan1@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 16:53:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Add a resource-dependent task duration plugin

Scylla gives an activity a single duration regardless of who performs it.
Process models discovered by Simod in differentiated mode give every resource
its own distribution per activity -- up to 42 per activity on BPIC 2012, with
the fastest and slowest more than a hundredfold apart.

The plugin reads a <resourceDurations> block inside a task and uses the
distribution belonging to the resource actually assigned. The node-level
<duration> stays as the fallback, so a build without the plugin runs the model
unchanged.

This needed a small core change, unlike the arrival calendar plugin. The
assigned resource is in scope when the duration is drawn -- TaskBeginEvent
samples at line 87 and reads the resource tuple at line 94 -- but
getDistributionSample() took only a node id, and TaskBeginEventPluggable fires
after the duration is already sampled. Doing it purely from an existing hook was
checked and is not possible: both resource-assignment paths (TaskEnableEvent for
an immediate start, SimulationModel when the task was queued) complete before
TaskBeginEvent runs, and TaskEnableEventPluggable fires while a queued task
still has no resource.

So ProcessSimulationComponents gains a resource-aware overload that consults a
registry a plugin fills, falling back to the node-level distribution when it is
empty, and TaskBeginEvent passes the tuple it already holds. Default behaviour
is unchanged for any model without the new element.

The lookup also tries the part of an instance name after "__", so a converter
that pools several resources into one dynamicResource can still identify the
original resource. Scylla names running resources by instance name, which
otherwise loses that.

Measured on BPIC 2012 at 500 cases: with the plugin, mean cycle time drops from
0.84x Prosimos to 0.47x. That is the opposite of the intended direction and is
not a defect -- Scylla assigns whichever pool instance is free, so fast
resources finish sooner, free up sooner, and take disproportionately more work.
On W_Completeren aanvraag the declared resource means run 6.7 s to 1060.7 s with
a median of 343.8 s, while the realised median is 182 s. Prosimos allocates
near-uniformly instead. The engines differ in how they choose a resource, and
per-resource durations make that visible rather than causing it.
